body:
Index describes one file: Index::write stamps that file's length and mtime, and the free fn is_current re-stats one data path against one index path. So Aggregate<IndexedFasta> carries one Index per part, and the only thing the aggregate adds is starts, one usize per part. Nothing on disk records that the parts belong together, and a record's global index depends on the order they were passed in, so that order exists only in the call that built the aggregate.

The proposal is a second index type for the aggregate: an ordered list of parts, each with a path, a stamp and a record count. Given the counts, starts falls out without opening a part at all.

    impl Parts {
        pub fn build(paths: &[PathBuf], format: Format) -> Result<Self>;
        pub fn read(path: &Path) -> Result<Self>;
        pub fn write(&self, path: &Path) -> Result<()>;

        /// The parts, in the order that fixes the global index.
        pub fn paths(&self) -> &[PathBuf];

        /// Each part's first global position, with the total on the end.
        pub fn starts(&self) -> &[usize];

        /// Which parts no longer match the stamp recorded for them.
        pub fn stale(&self) -> Result<Vec<usize>>;
    }

stale answering with positions rather than a bool is what decides the cost at a thousand files: one edited file should rebuild one part.

Parts is a placeholder name, not a proposal. Index is taken and means one file's offsets, and if the type only points at the per-file indexes then a name implying it holds offsets is wrong. The name is settled with the inline-or-by-reference decision.
